---
import { Code } from "astro:components";
import {
  ArrowLeft,
  BellRing,
  Calculator,
  CloudUpload,
  ExternalLink,
  FileText,
  LayoutDashboard,
  Mail,
  Repeat,
} from "@lucide/astro";
import Layout from "@/layouts/Layout.astro";
import SocialIcon from "@/components/SocialIcon.astro";
import Cover from "./components/_Cover.astro";
import { Badge } from "@/ui/badge";
import { Button } from "@/ui/button";
import { Card, CardContent, CardHeader, CardTitle } from "@/ui/card";
const repoUrl = "https://github.com/NandiyaLive/quoteflare";
const demoUrl = "https://quote.neranjana.me";
const deployUrl = `https://deploy.workers.cloudflare.com/?url=${repoUrl}`;
const tags = ["Cloudflare Workers", "D1", "TanStack Start", "React"];
const parts = [
  {
    title: "Pricing calculator",
    icon: Calculator,
    text: "A visitor picks services, options and quantities. The server recomputes the total from current prices, so the client cannot change it.",
  },
  {
    title: "Quotes to invoices",
    icon: FileText,
    text: "A submitted quote becomes an invoice numbered like INV-2026-0001. Advance payments are supported.",
  },
  {
    title: "Email",
    icon: Mail,
    text: "Invoices go out through Resend with a link to a public page. A webhook records delivery, bounces and complaints.",
  },
  {
    title: "Reminders",
    icon: BellRing,
    text: "A daily job marks unpaid invoices overdue and can email clients before and after the due date.",
  },
  {
    title: "Recurring invoices",
    icon: Repeat,
    text: "Schedules create, and optionally send, invoices weekly, monthly, quarterly or yearly.",
  },
  {
    title: "Admin dashboard",
    icon: LayoutDashboard,
    text: "Clients, services, packages, quotes, invoices and settings, behind Cloudflare Access.",
  },
];
const quickStart = `git clone ${repoUrl}.git
cd quoteflare
bun install
cp .dev.vars.example .dev.vars
bun run db:migrate:local
bun run db:seed
bun run dev`;
---

<Layout
  title="Quoteflare"
  image="/projects/quoteflare/og.png"
  description="A self-hosted invoicing app for one-person businesses, running on Cloudflare's free tier."
>
  <main id="main-content" class="page-main container max-w-4xl pt-24 pb-16">
    <Button as="a" href="/projects" variant="outline" size="sm" class="mb-8">
      <ArrowLeft />
      Back to Projects
    </Button>
    <article>
      <Cover />

      <header class="my-10 space-y-4">
        <h1 class="space-y-4">
          <span class="block text-4xl font-bold">Quoteflare</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-muted-foreground text-lg">
          Quotes and invoices for a one-person business, running on Cloudflare's
          free tier.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2">
          {tags.map((tag) => (
            <Badge variant="secondary">{tag}</Badge>
          ))}
        </div>
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3 pt-2">
          <Button
            as="a"
            href={repoUrl}
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            class="rounded-full px-6"
          >
            <SocialIcon platform="GitHub" class="size-4" />
            View on GitHub
          </Button>
          <Button
            as="a"
            href={demoUrl}
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            variant="secondary"
            class="rounded-full px-6"
          >
            <ExternalLink class="size-4" />
            Live Demo
          </Button>
          <Button
            as="a"
            href={deployUrl}
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
            class="rounded-full bg-[#fd6503] px-6 text-white hover:bg-[#e35a02]"
          >
            <CloudUpload class="size-4" />
            Deploy to Cloudflare
          </Button>
        </div>
      </header>

      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Quoteflare is a self-hosted invoicing app for a single owner. Visitors
          build a quote with a public pricing calculator. You turn the quote
          into an invoice, email it to the client, and let the app chase late
          payments.
        </p>
        <p>
          It runs as one Cloudflare Worker with a D1 database and an R2 bucket,
          so a small business can host it without paying for servers. It does
          not take payments. Invoices carry a status that you set yourself.
        </p>
        <h2>What it does</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-5 grid grid-cols-1 gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
        {parts.map(({ title, icon: Icon, text }) => (
          <Card class="border-border bg-card/40 gap-2 shadow-none backdrop-blur-sm">
            <CardHeader>
              <CardTitle>
                <h3 class="flex items-center gap-2 text-xl">
                  <Icon class="text-muted-foreground size-5 shrink-0" />
                  {title}
                </h3>
              </CardTitle>
            </CardHeader>
            <CardContent>
              <p class="text-muted-foreground">{text}</p>
            </CardContent>
          </Card>
        ))}
      </div>

      <div class="prose mt-8">
        <p>
          Behind the calculator sits a service catalogue with categories, fixed
          or custom pricing, weekly to yearly billing, option groups, required
          services and packages. You can import a whole catalogue from JSON.
        </p>

        <h2>Tech stack</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>TanStack Start</strong> — React 19 framework and routing.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Cloudflare Workers</strong> — Runs the app and the daily
            cron.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Cloudflare D1</strong> — Database, queried with Drizzle ORM.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Cloudflare R2</strong> — Stores the logo.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Hono</strong> — Serves <code>/api</code>.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Resend</strong> — Sends email and reports delivery.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>React Email</strong> — Renders email templates.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Cloudflare Access</strong> — One-time PIN login for{" "}
            <code>/admin</code>.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Tailwind CSS 4 and shadcn/ui</strong> — Styling and
            components.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Vitest</strong> — Runs the tests.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Design notes</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            The browser sends only the choices a visitor made. The server reads
            current prices from D1 and computes the total, and Turnstile checks
            the submit.
          </li>
          <li>
            Money is stored in cents and percentages in basis points, so every
            amount is an integer.
          </li>
          <li>
            One daily cron run does three jobs in order. It marks overdue
            invoices, sends reminders, then creates recurring invoices.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Limits</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>It has one owner. Access allows a single email.</li>
          <li>It takes no payments. You set each invoice's status yourself.</li>
          <li>Reminders stay off until you turn them on in settings.</li>
          <li>Sending email needs a domain verified in Resend.</li>
          <li>
            The Cloudflare free plan allows 100,000 Worker requests, 5 million
            D1 rows read and 100,000 D1 rows written per day.
          </li>
          <li>
            A daily run handles at most 5 recurring schedules to stay within the
            free plan's subrequest limit. The rest wait for the next day.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Deploy it</h2>
        <p>
          The Deploy to Cloudflare button above copies the repository to your
          GitHub account, creates the D1 database and R2 bucket, and deploys the
          Worker to <code>workers.dev</code>. It asks for the secrets listed in{" "}
          <code>.dev.vars.example</code>. Then:
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            Run <code>bunx wrangler d1 migrations apply DB --remote</code> from
            a clone of your copy.
          </li>
          <li>
            Set <code>ACCESS_TEAM_DOMAIN</code>, <code>TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY</code>
            , <code>EMAIL_FROM</code>, <code>APP_ORIGIN</code> and{" "}
            <code>ALLOWED_ORIGINS</code> in <code>wrangler.toml</code> and push.
          </li>
          <li>
            Create a Cloudflare Access application for <code>/admin/*</code>{" "}
            with one allow policy for your email, then set{" "}
            <code>ACCESS_AUD</code> to its audience tag.
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          Until the third step is done, <code>/admin</code> rejects every
          request. The GitHub guide covers deploying by hand, the email webhook
          and backups.
        </p>

        <h2>Run it locally</h2>
        <p>
          You need Node.js 22.12 or newer and{" "}
          <a href="https://bun.sh" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
            Bun
          </a>
          .
        </p>
        <Code
          code={quickStart}
          lang="bash"
          themes={{ light: "github-light", dark: "github-dark" }}
        />
        <p>
          The app runs at <code>http://localhost:3000</code>. Use{" "}
          <code>bun run dev</code>, not <code>bun --bun run dev</code>, which
          breaks the Cloudflare plugin. The example env file uses Cloudflare's
          always-pass Turnstile test keys, so add a <code>RESEND_API_KEY</code>{" "}
          to send real email.
        </p>

        <h2>Source</h2>
        <p>
          Quoteflare is an open source project released under the MIT License.
          It is an independent project and is not affiliated with, endorsed by
          or sponsored by Cloudflare. The code, the deploy steps and a full
          guide are on{" "}
          <a href={repoUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
            GitHub
          </a>
          . Issues and pull requests are welcome.
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>
  </main>
</Layout>
